Create a high-end fashion editorial portrait poster where the subject and typography share the same visual space rather than looking like text placed over a photo.

Use one adult fashion subject with a clean elongated silhouette, realistic skin and hair, monochrome clothing, natural fabric folds, dimensional studio lighting, and a restrained editorial pose.

Build the composition around oversized ultra-condensed sans-serif typography. The main word should be extremely large, partially cropped, and function like an architectural element.

Combine bold solid type, thin outline type, cropped letterforms, and clear foreground/background overlaps. Let some letters sit behind the subject while selected outlines or shapes pass in front to create real depth.

Use [TYPE MECHANISM] as the main visual idea:
• Negative space: typography disappears inside a clean geometric zone around the subject.
• Broken type: split the word with a deliberate gap and place the subject inside it.
• Type doorway: turn giant letters into walls or a passage around the subject.
• Expanded outline type: use widely spaced outline letters and generous negative space.

Keep the background minimal. Add only a few meaningful pieces of microcopy and one small high-saturation [ACCENT COLOR] label containing [ACCENT LABEL].

Maintain a strong hierarchy: one dominant typographic gesture, one subject, one secondary outline layer, one small color accent, and controlled negative space.

The final image should feel like contemporary fashion editorial art direction: minimal, bold, graphic, photographic, spatial, and stop-scroll worthy.

